<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-derivatives-of-polynomials" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Derivatives of Polynomials</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.2 Differentiation I
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>
                        Calculate the gradient at a curve at point
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    8 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Discover geometrically that the slope of a sum <m>h = f + g</m> at any point equals the sum of the slopes of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> separately.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Emphasise the "stacking" idea: the height of <m>h(x)</m> is just <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> added together. Ask learners to conjecture whether the <em>slopes</em> of the tangent lines also add. Do not state the sum rule until after the activity.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="activity-stacking-functions">
        <title>Stacking Functions</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>f(x) = x^2</m> and <m>g(x) = 2x</m>. Define <m>h(x) = f(x) + g(x) = x^2 + 2x</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The diagram below shows all three curves. Notice that the height of <m>h</m> at any <m>x</m> is exactly the sum of the heights of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> — you can think of <m>h</m> as <m>g</m> "stacked on top of" <m>f</m>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-stacking-functions">
                <caption>
                    <m>h = f + g</m>: the height of <m>h</m> is the blue height of <m>f</m> plus the green height of <m>g</m>. The short tangent segments show that the gradients add too: <m>f'(x) + g'(x) = h'(x)</m>.
                </caption>
                <interactive xml:id="interactive-stacking-functions" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/stacking-functions.js">
                    <slate xml:id="stacking-functions" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
                    <instructions>
                        <p>Drag the red cursor along the <m>x</m>-axis. The stacked bar shows <m>h = f + g</m>; the readout shows the slopes adding, <m>f'(x) + g'(x) = h'(x)</m>.</p>
                    </instructions>
                </interactive>
            </figure>

            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Use the power rule to find <m>f'(x)</m> and <m>g'(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(x) = 2x</m> and <m>g'(x) = 2</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate <m>f'(1)</m>, <m>g'(1)</m>, and their sum. What is the slope of the tangent to <m>h</m> at <m>x = 1</m>?
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(1) = 2</m>, <m>g'(1) = 2</m>, sum <m>= 4</m>.
                            Differentiating <m>h(x) = x^2 + 2x</m> directly gives <m>h'(x) = 2x + 2</m>, so <m>h'(1) = 4</m>. They match.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Repeat at <m>x = 2</m>: compute <m>f'(2)</m>, <m>g'(2)</m>, their sum, and <m>h'(2)</m>.
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(2) = 4</m>, <m>g'(2) = 2</m>, sum <m>= 6</m>. And <m>h'(2) = 2(2)+2 = 6</m>. ✓
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Use Sage to verify your answers and explore other values of <m>x</m>.
                    </p>
                    <sage>
                        <input>
var('x')
f = x^2
g = 2*x
h = f + g

print("Derivatives:")
print(f"  f'(x) = {diff(f, x)}")
print(f"  g'(x) = {diff(g, x)}")
print(f"  h'(x) = {diff(h, x)}")
print()
for a in [1, 2, -1]:
    fp = diff(f, x)(x=a)
    gp = diff(g, x)(x=a)
    hp = diff(h, x)(x=a)
    print(f"At x = {a}:  f'={fp}, g'={gp}, f'+g'={fp+gp}, h'={hp}")
                        </input>
                    </sage>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Based on your results, conjecture: if <m>h(x) = f(x) + g(x)</m>, what is <m>h'(x)</m> in terms of <m>f'(x)</m> and <m>g'(x)</m>?
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            Learners should conjecture <m>h'(x) = f'(x) + g'(x)</m>. This is the sum rule.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                    <note component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            Ask: "Does this make geometric sense? If both <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are rising steeply, is it surprising that their sum rises even more steeply?" Encourage learners to think of the tangent slope as a rate: if one quantity grows at rate <m>f'</m> and another at rate <m>g'</m>, the combined quantity grows at rate <m>f' + g'</m>.
                        </p>
                    </note>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Suggested Time</title>
                    <p>
                        5 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Objective</title>
                    <p>
                        State the sum rule and constant multiple rule, then show how they combine with the power rule to give a one-pass algorithm for differentiating any polynomial.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Connect to the activity:</alert> the pattern learners found — that the slope of <m>h = f + g</m> is <m>f' + g'</m> — is the sum rule. It follows from the limit of a sum being the sum of the limits.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The constant multiple rule is the same idea: a constant just scales the function, so it scales the derivative too. Constants factor out of limits.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Together with the power rule, these two rules reduce differentiation of any polynomial to a routine procedure: bring the exponent down, reduce by one, multiply any coefficient through.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-polynomial-rules">
        <title>Two Rules for Building Derivatives</title>
        <p>
            Both rules follow from basic properties of limits.
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>Sum Rule.</alert> If <m>h(x) = f(x) + g(x)</m>, then
            <md>h'(x) = f'(x) + g'(x).</md>
            The derivative of a sum is the sum of the derivatives.
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>Constant Multiple Rule.</alert> If <m>p(x) = c \cdot f(x)</m> for a constant <m>c</m>, then
            <md>p'(x) = c \cdot f'(x).</md>
            A constant factor multiplies through the derivative unchanged.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <p>
        Together with the power rule, these two rules let us differentiate any polynomial in a single pass: differentiate each term separately, multiplying the coefficient by the exponent and reducing the exponent by one.
    </p>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-poly-diff-basic">
        <title>Differentiating a Polynomial</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Find <m>f'(x)</m> for <m>f(x) = 4x^3 - 3x^2 + 2x - 7</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Apply the sum rule and constant multiple rule term by term, then the power rule to each term:
                <md>
                    <mrow>f'(x) \amp= \frac{d}{dx}(4x^3) + \frac{d}{dx}(-3x^2) + \frac{d}{dx}(2x) + \frac{d}{dx}(-7)</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= 4 \cdot 3x^2 - 3 \cdot 2x + 2 \cdot 1 + 0</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= 12x^2 - 6x + 2</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-poly-gradient-at-point">
        <title>Gradient at a Specific Point</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Find the gradient of <m>y = x^5 - 2x^3 + x</m> at <m>x = 1</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Differentiate term by term:
                <md>\frac{dy}{dx} = 5x^4 - 6x^2 + 1</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Substitute <m>x = 1</m>:
                <md>\frac{dy}{dx}\bigg|_{x=1} = 5(1)^4 - 6(1)^2 + 1 = 5 - 6 + 1 = 0</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                The gradient is <m>0</m>, so the tangent to the curve at <m>x = 1</m> is horizontal.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="PRECALC-ATdgOiokWi" original="PRECALC/modules/m49455/index.cnxml" license="CC-BY-4.0">
        <title>Derivative of a Polynomial at a General Point</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Find the derivative of <m>f(x) = x^2 - 3x + 5</m> at <m>x = a</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Differentiating term by term using the sum rule, constant multiple rule, and power rule:
                <md>f'(x) = 2x - 3</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Substituting <m>x = a</m>:
                <md>f'(a) = 2a - 3</md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-poly-diff">

        <exercise xml:id="PRECALC-XQH7bs526U" original="PRECALC/modules/m49455/index.cnxml" license="CC-BY-4.0">
            <statement>
                <p>Find the derivative of <m>f(x) = 3x^2 + 7x</m> at <m>x = a</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>f'(a) = 6a + 7</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-poly-2">
            <statement>
                <p>Find <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx}</m> for <m>y = 5x^4 - 3x + 8</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 20x^3 - 3</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-poly-3">
            <statement>
                <p>Find <m>f'(x)</m> for <m>f(x) = 2x^3 + x^2 - 4</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>f'(x) = 6x^2 + 2x</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-poly-4">
            <statement>
                <p>Find <m>f'(2)</m> for <m>f(x) = x^3 - 3x</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>f'(x) = 3x^2 - 3</m>, so <m>f'(2) = 3(4) - 3 = 9</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-poly-5">
            <statement>
                <p>Find all values of <m>x</m> where the gradient of <m>y = x^2 - 4x</m> is zero.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2x - 4 = 0</m>, giving <m>x = 2</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-poly-double-root">
            <title>Double Roots and Derivatives</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Let <m>p(x) = x^3 - 3x + 2</m>.</p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Use the factor theorem to show that <m>x = 1</m> is a root of <m>p(x)</m>. Then factorise <m>p(x)</m> fully and identify any repeated roots.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Find <m>p'(x)</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Evaluate <m>p'(1)</m>. What does this tell you about the tangent to <m>y = p(x)</m> at <m>x = 1</m>?</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Find the other root of <m>p(x)</m> from your factorisation. Is <m>p'</m> equal to zero there? What does this suggest about simple roots versus double roots?</p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>p(1) = 1 - 3 + 2 = 0</m>, confirming <m>x = 1</m> is a root. Dividing:
                            <md>p(x) = (x-1)(x^2 + x - 2) = (x-1)(x-1)(x+2) = (x-1)^2(x+2).</md>
                            So <m>x = 1</m> is a double root and <m>x = -2</m> is a simple root.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>p'(x) = 3x^2 - 3</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>p'(1) = 3(1)^2 - 3 = 0</m>. The tangent at <m>x = 1</m> is horizontal — the curve just touches the <m>x</m>-axis and turns back without crossing.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The other root is <m>x = -2</m>. <m>p'(-2) = 3(4) - 3 = 9 \neq 0</m>. At a simple root the curve crosses the <m>x</m>-axis with a non-zero gradient, but at a double root the tangent is horizontal and the curve only touches the axis.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

    </exercises>

